<section id="about" class="py-24 bg-slate-50">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold text-teal tracking-widest uppercase mb-8">About</h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I started as a software engineer before moving into support engineering — not because
          I wanted out of the code, but because the intersection of technical depth and customer
          empathy turned out to be genuinely interesting. Engineers who've shipped code understand
          customer pain differently. They know what it feels like when something breaks at the
          worst possible time, and they know how to talk to the people who built it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm currently Head of Support Engineering at
          <a
            href="https://sourcegraph.com"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal hover:text-teal-dark underline underline-offset-2 decoration-teal/40 transition-colors"
          >Sourcegraph</a>,
          leading a global team of ten supporting one of the most technically complex developer
          tools on the market — code search, on-prem and cloud, with dedicated white-glove support
          for enterprise customers. Before that, I spent years at
          <a
            href="https://saucelabs.com"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-teal hover:text-teal-dark underline underline-offset-2 decoration-teal/40 transition-colors"
          >Sauce Labs</a>,
          starting as a support engineer and working up to Senior Manager of the Escalation team —
          player/coach the whole way, personally resolving the hardest issues for our largest
          accounts while building the team and systems around them.
        </p>
        <p>
          My customers have always been engineers: software engineers, DevOps engineers, QA
          engineers, engineers in test. That shapes how I hire, how I train, and what I expect
          from a support team.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
